### Reading this LLVM program from zero

Do not read every displayed block as one straight-line script. A call supplies the function inputs and starts its first block. Within the selected block, follow the written instructions until a terminator chooses another block or returns to the caller. A label names a block; it does not execute an operation by itself.

| Spelling in these listings | How to read it here |
| --- | --- |
| `define i32 @count_to(i32 %limit) {` | `define` starts a function body. `@count_to` is the module-level function name; `i32` immediately before it is the return type. Parentheses contain typed inputs supplied by the caller; braces contain its blocks. |
| `i1` / `i32` | i1 means one bit: 0 is false and 1 is true for a condition; i32 means a 32-bit integer bit pattern; signed interpretation comes from operations such as slt or sdiv, not from a separate signed i32 type. |
| `ptr` | An opaque pointer value: it can represent an address or the special handle returned in the coroutine example. It does not contain an i32 integer or encode a pointee type. A load/store spells out the type of value accessed separately from the pointer. |
| `%i = ...` | The left side names the result of this instruction. Each SSA result has one static defining instruction; input arguments are values supplied at function entry. A name is a handle for a value, not a mutable source variable or a guaranteed CPU register. |
| `entry:` and `label %entry` | The colon defines a block label; the label operand names a jump destination. `%` is also used for local result/argument names, so use the type/context to distinguish a value from a block target. `@` instead identifies a module-level symbol. |
| `i32 0` | This is a typed literal: the i-prefixed part specifies the bit width, and the decimal part is the constant value. For example, a case number or stored state ID is an integer value, unlike a `%name` or `name:` label. |
| `; preds = ...` / other `;` text | Everything after a semicolon on that line is a comment for the reader. The printer lists incoming blocks after preds; it is not an instruction, an execution order, or a separate edge definition. Branch/switch operands actually define the edges. |
| `align 4` | Alignment is in bytes, not elements, bits, or a value to store. An aligned alloca provides suitably aligned storage; an aligned load/store promises that its pointer meets that alignment. It does not initialize memory or change the integer read/written. |

### Every instruction used in this pair

| Instruction and an actual line | Meaning and operand order in these listings |
| --- | --- |
| `add`: `%next = add i32 %i, 1` | Add the two same-typed operands and define the sum. In these unflagged i32 examples, arithmetic is modulo 2^32; the test inputs stay in the small intended range. This calculates data, such as the next loop counter or x + 1, rather than choosing a block. |
| `alloca`: `%ms.state = alloca i32, align 4` | Reserve storage for the shown element type in this function invocation and produce its address, not the stored integer. `alloca i32` reserves one i32 slot. The allocation does not initialize its contents; a store must supply a value before an intended read. |
| `br`: `br label %header` | `br label %B` always transfers control to block B. `br i1 %C, label %A, label %B` goes to A for true and B for false; their order matters. A branch is the block’s final instruction and produces no result. It does not execute both destinations. |
| `icmp`: `%continue = icmp slt i32 %i, %limit` | Compare two same-typed integers and produce i1, which can feed br or select. `slt` asks whether the left operand is smaller using signed integer interpretation, so a negative i32 is below zero. The comparison computes a condition; it does not jump by itself. |
| `icmp slt`: `%continue = icmp slt i32 %i, %limit` | Read left-to-right: compare the first integer operand with the second, then use the resulting i1 as a condition. For the loop it compares the current counter with the bound; for the discount kernel it checks whether the amount is negative. |
| `load`: `%i.reload = load i32, ptr %i.reg2mem, align 4` | Read a value from memory: in `load i32, ptr %slot`, i32 is the loaded value’s type and %slot is its address. The left-hand result gets the current contents, not another copy of the address. Each load defines a new SSA value; it does not compute the value that a missing earlier store should have supplied. |
| `phi`: `%i = phi i32 [ 0, %entry ], [ %next, %body ]` | Define one value by the edge just used to enter this block. Each `[ VALUE, %PREDECESSOR ]` pairs an incoming value with its source block; it is not an array access. The PHI’s i32 type applies to every incoming value, so a literal inside a bracket does not repeat the type. PHIs go at the start of a block. On a loop’s first entry choose its entry value, and on a back edge choose its carried value. Multiple PHIs select incoming values together, rather than sequentially overwriting each other. |
| `ret`: `ret i32 %i` | End this function invocation and return the typed operand to its caller. `ret i32 %v` returns the integer value, while `ret ptr ...` returns a pointer. A return does not proceed to the next printed block or automatically go back to dispatch. |
| `select`: `%ms.next = select i1 %continue, i32 1, i32 2` | Produce a value from `select i1 CONDITION, TYPE TRUE_VALUE, TYPE FALSE_VALUE`. A true condition chooses the first alternative, false the second; alternatives have the same type. This does not branch or conditionally execute the instructions that computed the operands. Here the chosen result is either a destination’s state number or the final return value. |
| `store`: `store i32 0, ptr %i.reg2mem, align 4` | Write memory: in `store i32 VALUE, ptr ADDRESS`, the first operand is the value and the second is the destination address. A store has an effect but no result name. Writing a state slot changes the next routing choice; writing a demotion slot transports an original program value. |
| `switch`: `switch i32 %ms.current, label %ms.invalid [` | Transfer control according to one integer value. The destination immediately after the condition is the default for no matching case. Each line inside `[...]` pairs a typed case constant with `label %DESTINATION`; only one destination is taken. An original switch chooses a program outcome; a dispatcher switch decodes the stored state ID. The brackets are a case list, not a runtime array. |
| `unreachable`: `unreachable` | End the block by asserting that execution never reaches this point on a defined run. Actually executing it is undefined behavior; it is not error recovery, a return, or a C++ exception. The state-machine invariant must prevent a bad stored ID from taking this default path. |

Keep address, contents, and loaded value separate. For example, an alloca result is the address of a slot; a store writes an integer into that slot; a later load defines a new SSA name for the integer read back. The pointer name does not become the stored number. A value slot preserves program data, whereas ms.state (when present) stores a control-flow destination encoding.

### Walk one concrete execution

Call count_to with limit = 2. The initial counter is 0; the signed-less-than test is true for 0 and 1, so the body increments it to 1 and then 2. At 2 the test is false and exit returns 2. A PHI selects initial 0 on entry and the previous body’s next value on a back edge; after demotion the loop-variable slot carries those same values. The complete after listing stores state 0 for header, 1 for body, or 2 for exit. State storage controls where to run; separate value slots carry the counter. The default unreachable path is never an intended outcome.

<details>
<summary>LLVM IR — Read the complete before-and-after LLVM modules</summary>

### Before — loop original

```llvm
define i32 @count_to(i32 %limit) {
entry:
  br label %header

header:                                           ; preds = %body, %entry
  %i = phi i32 [ 0, %entry ], [ %next, %body ]
  %continue = icmp slt i32 %i, %limit
  br i1 %continue, label %body, label %exit

body:                                             ; preds = %header
  %next = add i32 %i, 1
  br label %header

exit:                                             ; preds = %header
  ret i32 %i
}
```

### After — loop full

```llvm
define i32 @count_to(i32 %limit) {
entry:
  %ms.state = alloca i32, align 4
  %i.reload.reg2mem = alloca i32, align 4
  %i.reg2mem = alloca i32, align 4
  store i32 0, ptr %i.reg2mem, align 4
  store i32 0, ptr %ms.state, align 4
  br label %ms.dispatch

header:                                           ; preds = %ms.dispatch
  %i.reload = load i32, ptr %i.reg2mem, align 4
  store i32 %i.reload, ptr %i.reload.reg2mem, align 4
  %i.reload.reload = load i32, ptr %i.reload.reg2mem, align 4
  %continue = icmp slt i32 %i.reload.reload, %limit
  %ms.next = select i1 %continue, i32 1, i32 2
  store i32 %ms.next, ptr %ms.state, align 4
  br label %ms.dispatch

body:                                             ; preds = %ms.dispatch
  %i.reload.reload1 = load i32, ptr %i.reload.reg2mem, align 4
  %next = add i32 %i.reload.reload1, 1
  store i32 %next, ptr %i.reg2mem, align 4
  store i32 0, ptr %ms.state, align 4
  br label %ms.dispatch

exit:                                             ; preds = %ms.dispatch
  %i.reload.reload2 = load i32, ptr %i.reload.reg2mem, align 4
  ret i32 %i.reload.reload2

ms.dispatch:                                      ; preds = %body, %header, %entry
  %ms.current = load i32, ptr %ms.state, align 4
  switch i32 %ms.current, label %ms.invalid [
    i32 0, label %header
    i32 1, label %body
    i32 2, label %exit
  ]

ms.invalid:                                       ; preds = %ms.dispatch
  unreachable
}
```

